  "notes": "West leads the ♣Q, which South wins in dummy. South almost certainly has to lose 3 heart tricks. The only other possible loser is the fourth round of diamonds.\n\nSouth can guard against some 4-2 diamond breaks by drawing only 2 rounds of trumps before playing on diamonds. If one of the defenders is short in both diamonds and spades South will be able to ruff his fourth diamond in the dummy.\n\nSouth's chances of success are better by playing for a dummy reversal. Almost all that is required is a 3-2 trump break. At trick 2, a club is played from dummy and ruffed high by South.\n\nSouth crosses to dummy in diamonds and ruffs another club high. South crosses again, this time in spades, and ruffs the last club high. Dummy still has a trump entry to draw the remaining trumps. 3 heart tricks are conceded at the end.\n\nA dummy reversal involves ruffing losers in the long trump hand. If the long trump holding can take enough ruffs so that it becomes shorter than short trump holding, an extra trick will be gained.",
